.docname {Simulation rules}
.include {docs}

This page describes exactly what happens during a turn. Every probability
mentioned here is configurable; see [Configuration](configuration.qd) for the
keys and their defaults.

## Turn order

Each turn starts by taking a snapshot of all creatures that existed at the
beginning of the turn. Creatures born during the turn do not act until the next
turn.

The simulation then runs these phases, in order:

1. Movement for all original turn creatures.
2. Combat for all surviving original turn creatures.
3. Removal of creatures killed in combat.
4. Procreation for all surviving original turn creatures.
5. Starvation for all surviving original turn creatures.
6. Removal of creatures killed by starvation.
7. Random food regeneration for board cells below the food cap.

Within each phase, creatures are processed in creation order.

The simulation stops when either all creatures are extinct or the configured
turn limit is reached.

## Movement

Aphids may move once per turn. If the move succeeds, an aphid picks one of the
eight surrounding directions at random.

Ladybugs may change their preferred direction set once per turn. A preferred
direction set holds three adjacent directions, such as northwest, north, and
northeast. If the move succeeds, a ladybug picks one of its three preferred
directions at random. This gives ladybugs a persistent heading, so they sweep
across the board instead of jittering in place.

.mermaid caption:{Aphids wander freely; ladybugs commit to a heading.}
    flowchart TD
        A[Creature acts] --> B{Aphid or ladybug?}
        B -- Aphid --> C[Any of 8 directions]
        B -- Ladybug --> D{Change heading?}
        D -- Yes --> E[New set of 3 adjacent directions]
        D -- No --> F[Keep current set]
        E --> G[Pick 1 of the 3]
        F --> G

### Edges

When a creature attempts to move outside the board, it bounces back into the
board instead of wrapping around. On a one-row or one-column board, the bounced
coordinate is clamped to the only valid coordinate.

## Combat

Combat can happen when aphids and ladybugs occupy the same cell.

An aphid can kill one ladybug in its cell. Its kill probability grows with the
number of other aphids sharing the cell:

$ p_{kill} = \min(1,\; p_{kill}^{aphid} + n_{others} \cdot p_{accomplice}) $

The acting aphid does not count as its own accomplice, and the final
probability is capped at `1.0`. A crowd of aphids is therefore genuinely
dangerous to a lone ladybug, while a single aphid rarely wins.

A ladybug can kill one aphid in its cell using the configured ladybug kill
probability, with no crowd bonus.

Killed creatures are removed after the combat phase, so they cannot procreate
or starve later in the same turn.

## Procreation

Aphids can procreate when more than one aphid occupies the same cell. A new
aphid is created in that cell with `2` life.

Ladybugs can procreate when more than one ladybug occupies the same cell. A new
ladybug is created in that cell with `5` life and a random preferred direction
set.

New creatures do not move, fight, procreate, or starve until the next turn.

| | Starting life | Newborn life |
|---|---|---|
| Aphid | `10` | `2` |
| Ladybug | `15` | `5` |

## Starvation and food

During starvation, each surviving original turn creature consumes from the food
value of its current cell.

- If the cell has food, the cell food value drops by `1` and the creature keeps
  its life for that turn.
- If the cell has no food, the creature loses `1` life.
- Food values never drop below `0`.

A creature dies when its life drops below `1`.

After starvation deaths are removed, each cell below the food cap has a
configured chance to regain `1` food. The default regeneration probability is
`0.1`, and food is capped at `9` per cell.

> Note: Food is the real constraint on population growth. A dense cluster of
> creatures strips its cells bare within a few turns and then starves, which is
> what produces the boom-and-bust cycles visible in the GUI's chart.

## Randomness

The simulation uses `rand::rngs::StdRng` through a small wrapper in
.repolink {src/lib.rs} {blob/main/src/lib.rs}.

Without a seed, the generator is seeded from operating-system randomness. With
a seed, the same starting configuration and seed produce the same sequence of
random decisions:

```sh
cargo run -- --turns 20 --delay-ms 0 --seed 12345
```

The GUI exposes the same mechanism through its Run setup section, and its undo
history restores the random-number state along with the board, so an undone
edit replays identically.
